[{:seon.task/name "no-head-of-line-blocking"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M, HEAD 6184c92521]: the api proc sends to an agent's proc as a Flow output (seon.system/to-agent, system.clj:308, step/out! to [id :in]); that in-port's buffer is :seon.config.agent/inbox-size 64 (system.clj:397, default.edn:287); the agent proc's own send to its socket no longer blocks (seon.agent-proc/to-proc!, agent_proc.clj:33, core.async put! onto the end's :out). Reproduced before that fix [M, 2026-10]: one busy agent plus one chatty one stalled root's api ~25 s while health said :running. Cause [H, Flow's output put not yet read in Jolt's flow/impl.clj]: Flow delivers a step's outputs with a parking put, so one agent proc whose 64 slots are full parks the api's whole step: every other agent, the writer and the web wait behind it (head-of-line). Design: the channel rule of docs/core-async.md: a stream that must arrive gets a bounded lossless buffer on a reader that never makes its producer wait on a slower one. The agent proc admits by credit: the api keeps per-agent credit (inbox-size, from config) in its state, sends only while credit remains, queues the rest per agent in its own state (a plain value), and the agent proc's :taken message returns credit. Health reports the oldest unanswered request per agent (progress), not liveness. Deletes: nothing kept beside it; the 64-slot buffer becomes the credit's size. Estimate: per message one map update in the api, O(1); no thread. Proofs, red first: seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another (an agent proc that reads nothing, 200 messages to it, a second agent's eval answered within its own work); design guard seon.main-test/no-root-proc-output-can-park-on-one-agent (every output port of the api to an agent proc is credit-bounded, read from the graph's declaration)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-busy-agent-never-stalls-another seon.main-test/no-root-proc-output-can-park-on-one-agent}}
 {:seon.task/name "start-takes-a-config-layer"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: needs root's gate (AGENTS §3) answered. Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.system/start! (system.clj:1792) takes :max-running, :provider and :stall-ms beside the config; seon.config/with-provider (config.clj:519) folds a provider in (system.clj:1837); bin/check and seon.e2e pass them; the config written on main's root entity is what the reconciler reads; seon.config/value reads the process-wide config, so two roots in one process (the checks') cannot differ. Cause: a root's config in force is passed as options, a second path beside the one resolver. Design (in line with the one start, 2026-11-14): a root's config is its branch's config entries, held at its one start (seon.program/hold-config!, the same act every process does, Stage 0.5's hold-env!); a check that wants other values writes them as config entries on its store's main before start!, never as options; two roots in one process is a check fixture only and goes once checks fork the running runtime (seon.test-store). Deletes: start!'s :max-running, :provider and :stall-ms options (stall-ms goes anyway with seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error), seon.config/with-provider. Never removes functionality: each option's caller moves to a config entry. Estimate: O(callers) edits; no runtime cost. Proof, red first: seon.system-test/a-roots-config-is-its-mains-entries (start! with no options on a store whose main states :seon.config.agent/max-running 2 runs 2); design guard seon.main-test/start-takes-no-config-option (start!'s schema names no config key)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-roots-config-is-its-mains-entries seon.main-test/start-takes-no-config-option}}
 {:seon.task/name "roots-log-is-bounded"
  :seon.task/plan "Curated 2026-11-15 by context (root's ruling: a task's work is never a bin/ shell script; bin/ is a thin wrapper over an op, AGENTS §0): the work is in the process that writes root's log (its owner bounds it, by config), not bin/system's truncation. Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: bin/system start truncates runs/root.log at each start (bin/system:101, `: > \"$LOG\"`), so the previous start's log, a crash's included, is lost; within one root's life nothing bounds it; bin/held --log writes it (bin/system:110). Cause: the log is a file the shell owns with no declared bound. Design: rotation at an event, never a timer: at start, root.log moves to root.log.1 (one previous kept); within a life, bin/held's log writer stops writing past :seon.config.root/log-bytes (a new spec'd key with unit and provenance in default.edn; default from the largest measured clean day's log x10) and writes one last line naming the bound reached, so reaching it is a typed, visible end, never a silent drop. Deletes: the truncation at bin/system:101. Shared machinery: run through bin/system start, bin/update and bin/test's teardown. Estimate: one rename per start, one size compare per write. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/a-start-past-the-bound-keeps-one-previous-log; seon.check-test/a-root-log-past-its-bound-says-so (bin/held with a 1 KB bound)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-start-past-the-bound-keeps-one-previous-log seon.check-test/a-root-log-past-its-bound-says-so}}
 {:seon.task/name "operator-commands"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: bin/system has start, stop, kill, status, next and run (usage, bin/system:199); one root per checkout on its ports (bin/ports); output to runs/root.log. v1's bin/seon also had open, logs and reset --force (~/src/seon/script/seon/operator.clj, read-only). Cause: parity gap (docs/parity.md). Design, thin wrappers only (principle 2): `bin/system open` opens the dashboard URL bin/ports names; `bin/system logs` tails runs/root.log; `bin/system reset` restarts root on a new main of the same store (:seon.config.root/main), the old main kept as a branch, nothing deleted (deleting is the owner's act). Each a line in bin/system's usage and docs/machine.md. These go with per-checkout systems at Stage 1.9's audit; reset is the one with lasting value (a fresh main without an erase). Estimate: reset costs a stop plus a boot on an empty main (~0.3 s stop measured, boot as today). Proof, red first: seon.system-test/reset-starts-a-new-main-and-keeps-the-old."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/reset-starts-a-new-main-and-keeps-the-old}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-merge-prunes-old-generations-and-check-logs"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.fx/build (fx.clj:762-785) makes a generation under :seon.config.source/generations-dir (default.edn:558), a worktree and a ~200 MB binary each; nothing removes old ones (the only remove-at caller, fx.clj:1275, removes a tree run's worktree); target/check/ logs grow likewise; docs/machine.md#disk-stays-bounded says delete by hand. Cause: a derived artifact with no owner of its lifetime. Design: the :build effect's owner, after a build answers, removes every generation but the running one and the one before (bin/worktree --remove-at), and check logs past :seon.config.check/kept-runs (a new spec'd key); a store holding a kept run is never removed. Under the one binary identity (deps' seon.fx/runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch) a generation is keyed by its build-input digest, so the kept set is the running digest and the one before. Deletes: the manual step in docs/machine.md. Estimate: one directory listing and k removes per build, O(generations). Proof, red first: seon.fx-test/a-build-leaves-the-running-generation-and-the-one-before."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.fx-test/a-build-leaves-the-running-generation-and-the-one-before}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-compute-executor-is-declared"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: no proc in src declares :workload :compute (git grep at HEAD), so Jolt's Flow compute-executor (an unbounded cached pool, 5 s compute-timeout) runs nothing of ours; seon.system-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares exists. Root's unattributed thread growth (lifecycle 25 to 29, scale 38 to 55) is unexplained [H: not the compute pool, since nothing uses it; candidates: io-threads per test child exit watch (agent.clj, poller/exit-chan) and per granted item (seon.repl/serve-item!), the server's thread per connection (server.clj:635)]. Design: first measure which threads grow (name them; count before and after a cycle). Then each grower moves to a fiber or its proc's in-port, and if :compute is ever used it is a declared fixed pool sized by a config key. Deletes: whatever thread-per-item it finds. Estimate: root's thread count after a cycle equals the declared sum exactly. Proofs: seon.system-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares (exists; must hold after cycles); seon.render-test/a-slow-render-step-is-recorded-not-abandoned is dropped (render is :mixed, no compute timeout reaches it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/root-threads-are-what-root-declares seon.system-test/root-threads-after-ten-cycles-are-what-root-declares}}
 {:seon.task/name "event-loss-is-counted"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: root's buffers are literals in code: events-in (a/sliding-buffer 8192) (system.clj:1845), each seon.system/events tap a sliding 1024 never untapped (system.clj:1975-1979), render's tap a sliding 4096 (render/proc.clj:1200), :prove-main-buf and :vectors-main-buf sliding 1 (system.clj:1877-1881). Swept 2026-11-14 (channel semantics, docs/core-async.md): the :main buffers are single-stream (newest subsumes: safe); events-in, the taps and render's events are best-effort beside an authoritative answer (seon.server/prove-streamed writes what a tap missed). Cause: loss is legitimate there but silent, and the sizes are not declared config (AGENTS §5 only documented config). Design: every buffer is declared in resources/seon/system.edn :chan-opts with its loss semantics (lossless, or sliding with what subsumes it), sized by spec'd config keys; a sliding buffer on a best-effort stream counts drops into the api's view (:events-dropped per tap), shown on the dashboard; a tap is untapped when its reader closes. Deletes: the four literal buffers in code. Estimate: one counter increment per drop, O(1). Proofs, red first: seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted; seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration; design guard seon.main-test/no-buffer-is-a-literal-in-src (a/sliding-buffer or a/dropping-buffer only from the declaration)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/a-dropped-event-is-counted seon.procs-test/root-buffers-come-from-the-declaration seon.main-test/no-buffer-is-a-literal-in-src}}
 {:seon.task/name "warm-exercises-the-render-and-request-path"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.system/warm! (system.clj:1086, private, run once before the fork server forks) runs d/q, d/pull, d/db-with and d/with on a speculated value of its own, seon.sock/frame, seon.print/print-value and a defn plus deftest through clojure.test; no render pass and no turn's request path (seon.turn/context-block), so an agent's first render and first turn compile cold. The fork server's warm-start (fork_server.clj:77) warms an agent's first write. Cause: warming is two hand-picked lists of paths (system and fork server), which drift from what an agent runs. Design [H, measure first]: rather than a third list, the fork server runs one scripted agent turn on a speculated seed value (render plus context-block plus one eval) as its warm step, between requests (task seon.fork-server/a-first-fork-waits-for-nothing-it-does-not-use), so warming is the real path, never a list; warm! keeps only what root itself runs. Estimate: first turn ticks equal second turn ticks within 10%. Proof, red first: seon.system-test/forked-agent-s-first-turn-costs-same-ticks."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/forked-agent-s-first-turn-costs-same-ticks}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.system/await-db (system.clj:2074) named arity asks the reconciler for its next quiet and answers :seon.system/quiet-before-true when pred is still false (proved by seon.procs-test/a-wait-on-main-is-answered-at-the-reconcilers-quiet); a stopped root answers (gone nil \"root stopped\") (system.clj:2095); the unnamed arity is ended only by a commit (its docstring: a fact another proc makes); judge! still puts with a/>!! on a 64-slot channel and answers nil (system.clj:2067, :judge chan 64 at :1853). Cause: two arities with two end rules; the unnamed one can hang its caller when the producer (vector proc, problems) is quiet. Design: one arity: a wait names what it waits on and its producer proc; it ends at that producer's quiet fact (every proc reports quiet with its last t, as the reconciler does) with :seon.system/quiet-before-true; at stop every open wait answers :seon.system/ended with cause :stop (the ruling: never \"root stopped\"); judge! goes through ask-edge (answered closed at once on a closed channel). Deletes: the unnamed arity, the \"root stopped\" string answers here, judge!'s a/>!!. Estimate: one ask per wait, one pred check per quiet report. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/await-db-pred-cannot-hold-quiet-root-answers (unnamed producer quiet, pred false: answered); seon.system-test/a-wait-at-stop-answers-ended."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/await-db-pred-cannot-hold-quiet-root-answers seon.system-test/a-wait-at-stop-answers-ended}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-put-to-a-dead-proc-is-an-error"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: blocking puts whose result is dropped: seon.system/judge! (system.clj:2067, a/>!! onto :judge, 64 slots); seon.web's handler toward the web proc (web.clj:235, :241, :248, :267); the fork server's event reader into ev-ch (fork_server.clj:390-391, result used for its loop only); agent.clj's socket reader and test-child result puts (agent.clj:72, :1292, :1298, :1334, :1357). The api's answer-down (system.clj:946) already answers what a step would send to a proc that is down. Cause: an edge puts with >!! instead of the one edge ask (seon.step/ask-edge, which answers closed at once); a proc that died leaves its in-chan open and the put parks once full. Design: every edge put is step/ask-edge or a put whose false is the caller's typed answer naming the proc (:seon.system/ended with cause :proc-down); a proc's death closes its in-channels (answer-down marks it ::down). Deletes: the bare >!! at those sites. Estimate: none added; one closed check per put. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/proc-stopped-full-channel-returns-false-caller-answers; design guard seon.main-test/an-edge-asks-one-way (exists: extend it to refuse a/>!! in src outside an edge reader thread)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/proc-stopped-full-channel-returns-false-caller-answers seon.main-test/an-edge-asks-one-way}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-named-wait-ends-when-a-runs-work-throws"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M at 2026-11-06, not re-read at HEAD]: seon.reconcile ends a flight whose seon.turn/work! threw (the :worked-error flight key), proved by the last block of seon.reconcile-test/a-death-names-its-cause; the named await-db then answers :seon.system/quiet-before-true at the reconciler's quiet. The six-hour hang (jolt/agent-loop before 89dca5cf3) was not re-run. Cause as filed: a run whose work! threw went on to :judge and :branch-diff, one of whose answers never came [H]. Design: nothing new; with the one-arity wait (task seon.system/a-wait-on-main-ends-when-nothing-can-make-it-true) and each request's own bound (seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error) no wait can outlive its bound. What is owed is the proof. Estimate: one in-agent run ~3M ticks. Proof, red first (revert the reconcile fix to see it red): seon.agent-test/a-run-whose-work-throws-ends-and-the-wait-answers. Likely done at HEAD apart from its proof: root may fold it into the wait task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-run-whose-work-throws-ends-and-the-wait-answers}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner: we may have merge conflicts or agents failing to merge, so make sure the tools to fix them by hand on root's side work as expected. What exists: the playbook is written, docs/merging.md#root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl, one entry for each of four failures with its working forms (staged and repaired once on a worktree root, 2026-10-27, with seon.fixtures/scenarios :hairy, :conflict and :semantic). Root has no conflict-resolving function of its own: my.branch/unmerged, resolve! and merge-main! run in an agent's process, so root's path is to attach to the held merge task. Of the gaps that staging found: built, (1) a worktree root commits on its own jolt/seon-merges-<worktree> (seon.config reads #seon/own-branch), (2) my.root/write-back!, (6) my.root/hold!, proved by seon.reconcile-test/root-takes-a-running-task-back-and-releases-it-again; filed as their own tasks, 3, 4 and 7 as seon.system/a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact, 5 found not to occur (an attach writes no :seon.task/model, docs/merging.md), 9 as root-repair-reads-are-cheap. Left here: gap 8 (my.root/cancel! leaves the task's problem entries open, and the page counts closed entries), not checked against the code as it is now; reproduce it first on a worktree root (never runs/store), fix it at its cause, and add what the playbook gains to its section."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/my-root-cancel-task-leaves-none-problem-entries}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-behind-merge-ends-in-a-fact"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 3, 4 and 7 of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl (staged 2026-10-27, to reproduce first: stage fixtures :conflict, attach the merge task, merge main and resolve, then give main a new namespace with seon.source/text-tx). (a) A merge stays :behind forever after main gains a namespace: the form of a namespace new on main reaches the branch with :ns nil, so main differs at that key on every merge (`seon.merge/program-of` :ns read, merge.clj, or the carry in `seon.merge/merge-tx`, merge.clj); control: a change to an existing namespace merges on the second run. Fix at the carry; check a namespace made from an agent's REPL (the :newns scenario) the same way. (b) Nothing bounds :behind reruns: a :behind run ends green so max-merge-turns never counts it (reconcile.clj ends the run :behind and the task stays ready); under a model that loops forever and root causes (quiescence only) never names it. A :behind of a branch that already merged main at the head it was refused at is a defect fact, and the task is held with that evidence. (c) A behind merge branch is judged before main is merged: an attached agent's REPL runs main's program while its branch db holds the old base, so the proofs pass on the branch and the held reason reads proof-unknown. seon_done (or the judge) refuses a branch whose base is behind with the writer's :seon.merge/behind message and its fix before judging proofs, or attach to a merge task runs my.branch/merge-main! as the turn loop does. Proof to write: merge-main! followed by the octopus merge is not :behind when main gained a namespace; a :behind rerun at an unchanged head holds the task. Checked 2026-11-06: (a) is built and proved, in the direction a branch makes a namespace and main takes it, by seon.merge-test/a-merge-carries-the-namespace-of-a-form-it-carries (green); main making the namespace and the branch merging main (the scenario above) has no test. (b) is built as a count, not as unchanged-head: :seon.config.merge/max-behind (3) judgings of one merge task that end :behind hold it for root with the writer evidence (seon.reconcile-test/a-merge-behind-again-and-again-is-held-for-root, green); a rerun at an unchanged head is not held earlier. (c) is built for the explanation only: a behind branch is told \"behind N\" before proof-unknown (seon.reconcile-test/a-branch-behind-main-is-told-so-first, green); whether attach runs merge-main! was not checked. Tests and default.edn cite these as seon.system/a-behind-merge-is-bounded-and-merges-main-first, a name no task has."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/merge-main-followed-octopus-merge-behind-main-gained}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-repair-reads-are-cheap"
  :seon.task/plan "Gaps 9 and the smaller ones of root-repairs-a-failed-merge-from-its-repl: root's repair reads cost and say too little. (1) `my.root/problems` took 66 ms / 100 MB to 1,063 ms / 1.7 GB allocated on a store of about 25 tasks and `seon.server/task-summaries` about 560 ms / 850-915 MB a call. (2) The page names root causes only at quiescence, so a run that never ends shows 'a31 idle' and nothing links a cause to its run or task; the conflicts' content is not on the page. (3) A held merge appears twice in `my.root/problems` (:merge and :failed), and the footer counts closed entries. (4) After a successful write-back retry the task keeps its stale :seon.task/commit-error (retract it); a namespace with no file records a commit-error naming each key twice (reconcile.clj, unfiled-error). (5) A request with raw newlines answers one :seon.server/defect per line: a line that does not read as a map answers once, naming the rule one request per line, newlines escaped. Proof to write: contracted calls and bytes of one my.root/problems read on 25 tasks, flat in the conversation (a cost proof); one answer for a multi-line request. Checked 2026-11-06 at a root of 295 tasks with no runs: (1) my.root/problems 147 ms and 755 KB, seon.server/task-summaries 63 ms and 103 MB, far under the numbers above; the input that made them slow (tasks with run histories) was not staged. (5) is still so: a request of two lines sent to the socket answered two :seon.server/defect values, the second a contract error of seon.server/answer. (2), (3) and (4) were not checked."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/request-two-lines-sent-socket-answers-error-naming}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-eval-never-moves-main"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime (the api's record-agents and the writer). Needs the owner's go (it changes what main records of a live agent). What exists [M]: seon.system/record-agents (system.clj:98) writes an agent's facts to main whenever its observed-keys change (system.clj:86: :status :pid :mem-mb :running :branch :forked-ms :ended-ms :stalled); :running and :status change at every eval, so every eval of any agent moves main (measured 2026-11-06: +8 :max-tx across one (+ 1 1) where a root read moves it by 1); every agent hears main moved and redraws (15.5M ticks a move, measured 2026-10-29), work in agents squared. The named proof exists but cannot fail on this bug (it compares only pairs of reads that saw the same main). Cause: a live view (what runs now) written as main facts, a second producer of what the agent's own branch already records. Design: (1) :running and per-eval :status are the api's view only (the dashboard reads the view, as the turns tile does since cf0e8f234d), never main; main keeps spawn, end, death and stall, which the judge and other agents read; (2) the writer commits no transaction whose datoms are all no-ops (seon.writer/commit-batch). Deletes: :running and per-eval :status from observed-keys. Estimate: main's :max-tx moves by 0 across an eval; saves one commit plus N agents' redraws per eval. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/an-eval-moves-main-by-nothing (main's :max-tx before and after one eval); seon.writer-test/a-transaction-of-no-datoms-is-no-commit; the existing seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did made to fail when no two reads share a main."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/an-eval-moves-main-by-nothing seon.writer-test/a-transaction-of-no-datoms-is-no-commit seon.server-test/a-later-read-draws-no-page-cold-and-costs-what-the-one-before-did}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-that-dies-starting-answers-its-attach"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.system/end-agent (system.clj:403) answers the spawn of an agent that ends before its hello through answer-spawn (system.clj:164); a fork step that throws is only recorded (:step-failed, system.clj:839, no spawn-failed: see seon.fork-server/a-fork-step-that-throws-answers-every-waiting-ask). An agent whose seon.agent/init! (agent.clj:2146 at HEAD) throws was seen 2026-11-01 to leave its attach waiting for good; not staged since. Cause [H]: a throw in init! after the fork answered :forked but before hello; if the child exits, end-agent should answer; if it lingers (a parked thread holding it alive) nothing does. Design: init! is the one start (hold-env!'s first act, Stage 0.5): any throw in it is reported on the socket as the start's typed answer (:seon.process/start-failed with the error) and the child exits; the spawn's answer is that error; and the attach rides each request's own bound regardless. Estimate: one frame per failed start. Proof, red first: seon.system-test/attach-agent-init-throws-answers-dead-error-within (a test environment whose begin-run! throws; the attach answers within its bound)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/attach-agent-init-throws-answers-dead-error-within}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-running-root-spawns-the-binary-it-was-built-as"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime, with deps' one binary identity. What exists [M]: seon.vector/start! (vector.clj:415) starts its helper by path (helper-argv, this checkout's target/) through a raw ProcessBuilder (vector.clj:427), the one spawn outside seon.lib/sh and fork!; seen 2026-11-05: a rebuild in the tree replaced the binary under a running root and its children read a config the root's did not (32 problem rows, 5 kinds). Cause: a process is started by a path whose contents can change, not by the identity of what root runs. Design, per the 2026-11-14 ruling: the one start reads seon.fx/runtime-for (a digest of build inputs vs target/seon's; task seon.fx/runtime-for-reads-the-pins-from-the-branch) and a child of root is started from the binary of root's own digest (kept under its generation, never overwritten in place); the embed helper moves onto the effects owner's process-start primitive. Deletes: vector.clj's ProcessBuilder and its path guess. Estimate: one digest lookup per start. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/replacing-file-target-seon-leaves-running-root-s; design guard seon.main-test/no-process-execs-target-seon-but-through-runtime-for (the law seon.fx's task names)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/replacing-file-target-seon-leaves-running-root-s seon.main-test/no-process-execs-target-seon-but-through-runtime-for}}
 {:seon.task/name "shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: seon.system/start! passes Flow :stop-timeout-ms (cfg :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms 10000, system.clj:1888, default.edn:43), what flow/stop waits for a proc's exit; stop! (system.clj:2388) parks on the drain with no bound and reports a proc still running in its :graph; a stalled agent at a stop is killed (the :stalled arm, system.clj:929-937); root's own exit is bounded at the edge by bin/held's MS (:seon.config.root/stop-ms 3000, on main since 98494a4f08). Cause: a second shutdown clock inside Flow beside the edge bound. Design (one stop, one drain, agreed 2026-11-14): stop quiesces, each in-flight item ends by its own bound (task seon.system/a-stop-or-crash-resumes-from-one-resume-point-and-says-what-it-lost), so flow/stop has nothing left to wait on; a proc that does not exit is a recorded :platform problem naming it, and the edge bound (bin/held MS) is the one clock. Whether Jolt's Flow accepts no :stop-timeout-ms must be read first in flow/impl.clj [H]. Deletes: :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms and the :stop-timeout-ms argument. Estimate: a clean stop ~0.2 s (measured 0.19-0.29 s, 12 stops). Proof, red first: seon.system-test/proc-does-exit-stop-recorded-problem-naming-time. May merge into the resume task (same drain)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/proc-does-exit-stop-recorded-problem-naming-time}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-system-entity-and-root-as-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner, 2026-11-15 (docs/decisions.md#one-root-an-ordinary-agent-the-system-is-not-an-agent): root is an ordinary agent, the first, ancestor of every agent; the system process is not an agent and holds no internal process state; one shape for an agent, its env included. Verified [M root REPL, 2026-11-15]: the root agent {:seon.agent/key \"root\"} matches :seon.db/agent-start-fact plus :seon.agent/root (a ref to the system entity); the system entity {:seon.system/id :root} matches :seon.system/root-fact and holds only system facts; seon_eval agent=root runs in the system process (server.clj root-eval) and session and page evals are recorded under the system entity (server.clj:115, render/proc.clj:1184); the system process holds tree-runs, uncaught, held-index, doc indexes and vector kept-rows outside Flow procs, and the api's :agents beside the agents' facts. Names (data, notes data-system-entity-names-2026-11-15.md): not :seon.graph (the Flow declaration's namespace, seon/graph.clj; none of its code touches these facts); :seon.system/* stays because seon.system's code reads and writes them; only root is wrong: the id value :root -> :system, :seon.system/root-fact -> :seon.system/system-fact, :seon.agent/root goes (liveness from :seon.agent/boot); collected/retained/content-t, quiescent, tasks and signed-in take their own concepts' schemas on the same entity. Split (flow, notes flow-system-vs-root-agent-2026-11-15.md): tree-runs into seon.prove's runs; the api's :agents keeps only live process state (pid, end, inflight), the rest read from agent facts; uncaught stays a named edge; caches stay until the reactive core; seon_eval agent=root reaches the root agent's own REPL, root's powers are ops on the system through await-reply; the system keeps one :inspect op, recorded under the asking session as an op event; every eval is recorded by seon.repl/record-tx under an agent. Agents' setup is unchanged. Env: :seon.task/env becomes the selection of the agent's own attributes (branch, model, max-turns, holder); agent-value's reassembly goes. Erase: one, after landing 6 has replayed the 28 run-history branches onto main and the import (the files a fresh store loads) holds everything else. Owners: flow (split), web (ops, recording), data (names, schemas, erase), runtime (boot). Estimate: no cost change, renames and one recording path."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-boot-writes-the-systems-facts-on-seon-graph seon.system-test/root-answers-as-the-agent-keyed-root seon.schema-test/no-seon-agent-root-attribute}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-task-files-kind-is-dropped-at-load"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner's design ruling about kinds (a thing is a map; choosing is merge) comes first (below). What exists: every task file declares a kind (306 declarations write :seon.task/kind :work), and seon.system/task-files makes each declaration a :whole task through seon.template/request, dropping the written kind (\"the kind is derived here and never written in a file\"). So every task on a store reads :whole (331 of 331 on a store seeded from main 2026-11-10), and the code that decides by kind for :work, :design or :implement is never reached from a task file: seon.problems/uncited-tasks judged only :implement and so never fired (fixed on jolt/task-cites); seon.render.ui hides a status line unless :work or :whole; seon.ctx.status and seon.reconcile read :plan and :plan-before only for :design; seon.task/merges? treats :whole as merging itself.

What allowed it: the kind is written in the files and decided in the loader, two places for one fact, and the written one is read by nothing; the readers key on a kind the data no longer carries.

The change waits on the owner's design ruling about kinds (a thing is a map; choosing is merge), which the managing session brings after the restart: either the files stop writing a kind (306 lines go) and every reader of :work, :design and :implement is retired with it, or the kind becomes data the loader keeps. Not to be fixed piecemeal. Evidence for the ruling: this task, and the list of readers that decide by kind kept in the Context session's state.

Proofs, once ruled: a task file's declaration loads with the kind the ruling says, and no reader keys on a kind no task on the store holds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-root-that-took-a-landing-stops"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: the edge half is built and on main (98494a4f08): bin/system stop waits for root's exit within :seon.config.root/stop-ms (3000), past it bin/evidence into runs/stop-overran-<pid>/, the group killed, STOP OVERRAN :seon.system/stop-overran (bin/system:124-135); proved by seon.check-test/a-stop-that-never-answers-ends-at-its-bound. Left: defect (1), why a root that took a landing does not end on TERM. Seen 2026-11-10 (13 min) and 2026-11-14 (a test shard's root, 21 min CPU): threads looping in Chez's S_foreign_entry -> lookup -> dlsym with failing lookups. [H] Jolt's zlib-procs (host/chez/java/zlib.ss:173-181) bind under a mutex and never remember a failed bind; [M] against it, zlib binds in a fresh jolt. Cause not yet named: both ends not read. Design: read the next overran capture's Scheme-level dump (bin/evidence's SIGINFO) to name the looping foreign entry, then fix at its owner (deps if Jolt/Chez: a failed foreign bind is remembered and answered as an error once). The landing path that loads code live (load-changes!) is replaced by the program reaction under one derivation, which removes the in-place rebinding this hang was first tied to. Estimate: a stop well under 1 s at any uptime (measured 0.19-0.29 s idle). Proof, red first: seon.system-test/a-root-that-took-a-landing-stops-in-a-second (start, land a pin-moving change, stop: ROOT STOPPED within stop-ms)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-root-that-took-a-landing-stops-in-a-second}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-top-bar-names-the-checkout-the-system-runs-in"
  :seon.task/plan "Owners: runtime (the boot and catch-up), web (the bar). What exists [M]: :seon.system/source is written by an import (seon.system/import-of, system.clj:1143), whose fact carries {:root :branch :commit :tree}; when main already holds the read's digest it answers {:digest} alone and writes nothing, so a store cloned from another checkout keeps that checkout's :root and :branch; the bar (seon.render.ui/source-line) renders it. Cause: which checkout a system runs in is a fact of the boot, written by the program import. Design, with one derivation's one catch-up (seed, import and landing as one function): the boot writes its own checkout and branch on its boot fact (seon.system/booted-source already writes boot, digest and inputs), the catch-up writes only what the files moved, and the bar reads the boot fact; the standing system's bar reads what it runs: main plus merged tasks not yet in git (from the source fact and the branch). Deletes: import-of's digest-equal early answer as the bar's source. Estimate: one fact per boot. Proof, red first: seon.system-test/a-cloned-store-names-the-checkout-it-boots-in."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-cloned-store-names-the-checkout-it-boots-in}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-landing-keeps-the-agents-that-are-working"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. Mostly built [M]: my.root/landed! (root.clj:435) keeps root running when seon.system/restart-for (system.clj:1540) finds no reason: main holds the landed tree as data (bin/land's bridge wrote seon.system/landed-tx) and what the binary lacks is forms, config or schema entries, loaded in place by the writer's main commit (writer.clj:45, seon.program/load-changes! {:tests? false}) and by the fork server's :tx (fork_server.clj:197); bin/update calls it (bin/update:78) and restarts only on {:restart why}. Left: the proof, and the way it loads. Per the one-derivation ruling (2026-11-14), the landing is one catch-up and root's program follows main by the program reaction (load-diff! from the program it holds to main's, the same as an agent's start), not load-changes!; restart-for's classes become the reaction's answer, :restart only for a pin or binary change (task seon.system/a-landing-restarts-root-only-for-a-binary-change, which this can merge into). Estimate: a src-only landing costs load-diff! of its changed forms (~2.5M ticks a form, measured) and no restart. Proof, red first: seon.system-test/a-src-landing-keeps-a-live-agent-working (a live agent across a src-only landing answers its next eval; root then runs main's new form)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-src-landing-keeps-a-live-agent-working}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-start-that-sweeps-transacts-on-a-live-connection"
  :seon.task/plan "DONE in code at HEAD; its proof is owed. What exists [M]: seon.system's open (system.clj:1688-1702) compacts first, before any connection (pdb/compact-if-due!, \"first of all: no process holds the store yet\"), then opens, drops tree branches and sweeps (pdb/retain!); a sweep's freed pages are compacted at the NEXT boot, never between a release and a reopen (the comment cites context's two failed starts of 2026-11-11). Cause fixed: the compaction closed and renamed the LMDB file under a still-referenced connection. Owed: the red-first proof (seen red by moving the compaction back after the sweep). Estimate: two starts on a small store ~2 boots. Proof: seon.system-test/a-start-that-sweeps-transacts-on-a-live-connection (two starts on one store with :seon.config.store/keep-commits small enough that the second sweeps, then a transact). Root may remove the entry once the proof is green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-start-that-sweeps-transacts-on-a-live-connection}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stall-that-waits-on-the-system-is-the-systems"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: the agent proc's :stall? (agent_proc.clj:122) judges an agent stalled when a request is in flight, no frame for stall-ms and :fx-out is zero; :fx-out counts only effects asked over the agent's pipe (agent_proc.clj:100-101, :118), not the agent's asks to root's server over its socket (seon.server ops a turn sends). Seen 2026-11-12: root at 100% CPU 16 min, the agent blocked in read on its socket to root; the stall flagged the agent and took the agent's evidence, none of root's. Cause: what root owes an agent on its server socket is not counted where the stall is judged. Design, inside the deadline slice (seon.step/every-wait-past-a-bound-is-a-flow-message-from-the-one-timer): every request has its own bound on its owner; an agent's server ask is a request on root's api with its bound, so past it root's own evidence is taken (seon.system/stall-state, bin/evidence on root's pid) and the problem names root and the op; the :stall? exemption is deleted. Estimate: one put-after! per server request. Proof, red first: seon.agent-proc-test/a-quiet-agent-the-system-owes-is-the-systems-stall."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-proc-test/a-quiet-agent-the-system-owes-is-the-systems-stall}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-api-that-throws-on-every-message-spins-on-its-own-errors"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: runtime. What exists [M]: root's Flow error channel is piped into the api's :back; the api's :error arm (system.clj:905-919) answers every message its own throwing step took (::lost) through seon.system/answer-taken (system.clj:969), which since 0d1718d446 does not take back an answer that was already an error through its :dest. Still true by reading: an api whose step throws on every message throws again on the :error message itself; that throw makes one more :error, one message spinning for good (the 11-12 spin's shape without the :dest). Cause: the step that threw is the one that handles its own error. Design: an :error whose :pid is :api is recorded through a path that cannot throw (the view's root-errors window and a problem, plain data, no contract call), never answered again by the api's normal arms; a second :error from :api about an :error message is dropped as already recorded and counted. Deletes: nothing; narrows the arm. Estimate: one check per :error message. Proof, red first: seon.procs-test/an-api-that-throws-on-every-message-records-it-once."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.procs-test/an-api-that-throws-on-every-message-records-it-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-stop-or-crash-resumes-from-one-resume-point-and-says-what-it-lost"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner B10: prepared for power loss and a hard crash, and a proper shutdown that loses nothing and resumes from a proper resume point; data loss on power loss accepted, errors shown, a path to resume always. Upgrades are online first (a new runtime from the new pin starts beside the old on the same data and takes over); when that cannot work, proper shutdown, rebuild, migration, clean start. What exists [M]: the store opens MDB_NOSYNC|SAFENOSYNC and the flusher syncs after each commit seen; our LMDB fork's first writable opener restores to the newest steady mark (mdb.c:7130-7150); stop! (system.clj:2388) writes :seon.system/stopped durably, then drains with no bound; the api's stop transition answers pending and waits (gone nil \"root stopped\") (system.clj:1044-1045); previous-end (system.clj:1664) reads it or finds :unclean; seon.reconcile's boot-wakes (reconcile.clj:1345) wakes waiting agents; root's exit is bounded at the edge (bin/held MS, :seon.config.root/stop-ms, 98494a4f08). A clean stop and a crash lose the same in-flight evals, asks and effect children. Design: :seon.system/resume on main's system entity {boot t agents [key branch task-id outside?] asks [uuid]}. One stop, one drain (runtime, tests, web agreed 2026-11-14): (1) runtime: stop! quiesces first (no new turns, spawns, effects or prove batches), each item in flight ends by its own bound (the deadline slice), then the resume point and stop fact in one flushed transaction; every request still open answers :seon.system/ended widened with a cause (:stop, :crash), never \"root stopped\"; (2) tests: the prove service answers queued items :stopped (never cached) and refuses new ones; (3) web: bin/update:117 calls bin/system stop and nothing else. A boot after a clean end respawns model agents on their branches, marks outside agents :lost until attached, answers open asks; after an unclean end it derives the same value (live agents with no ended-ms, open asks: one AVET seek each) and records an :unclean-end problem naming what was lost, and after power loss deps' restore ids. Online takeover later deletes bin/held's MS. Deletes: the \"root stopped\" strings, boot-wakes alone (folded into the resume), Flow's :stop-timeout-ms and :seon.config.root/flow-stop-ms (with seon.system/shutdown-and-death-are-dataflow); corrects failure-points' MDB_UNSETTLED row. Estimate: resume derivation O(live agents + open asks) seeks; clean stop ~0.2 s plus the longest in-flight item's own bound. Deps owes the restore ids. Power loss is never simulated. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/a-clean-stop-writes-a-resume-point-and-the-boot-resumes-it, an-unclean-boot-derives-the-resume-point, a-clean-stop-waits-for-work-by-its-own-bound, a-killed-root-loses-nothing-committed, an-unclean-end-is-shown, a-wait-at-stop-answers-ended; design guard seon.main-test/no-answer-is-root-stopped (no string answer for an ended wait in src)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/an-unclean-boot-derives-the-resume-point seon.system-test/a-clean-stop-waits-for-work-by-its-own-bound seon.system-test/a-killed-root-loses-nothing-committed seon.system-test/a-clean-stop-writes-a-resume-point-and-the-boot-resumes-it seon.system-test/an-unclean-end-is-shown seon.main-test/no-answer-is-root-stopped}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.fx/every-effect-is-defined-by-one-schema-entry-and-answers-one-typed-answer"}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-sessions-files-are-a-transaction-on-its-agents-branch"
  :seon.task/plan "Plan Stage 1.4. Owners: runtime (the op and the agent), data (the transaction, seon.source/tx-of). What exists [M]: seon.system/prove-tree! (system.clj:2250) catches a checkout up onto a tree branch tree-<id> (seon.db/tree-branch?, db.clj:790), tracked by tree-runs, tree-enter! and tree-leave! (system.clj:2187-2191), dropped after the run and at the next boot (pdb/drop-trees!, system.clj:1696); seon.system/catch-up (system.clj:1377) with :fact? false is the import; my.branch/import! is on main and built on catch-up. Cause: a session's files enter as a throwaway branch per tree, beside the agent branch a session already holds (seon_attach). Design, in line with one derivation's one catch-up: catch-up of the session's changed paths writes one transaction on the session's persistent agent branch (keyed by its area), unchanged files write nothing, a file that does not read is answered and nothing is written; the agent's program follows by the program reaction (load-diff! from what it holds to the branch's). Deletes, after an audit to the owner: tree-branch, tree-runs, tree-enter!, tree-leave!, :drop-tree-branch, pdb/drop-trees! and prove!'s checkout argument; the import and the live route stay. Estimate: O(changed files) reads plus their forms' records; a one-file edit ~the file's forms. Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/an-import-of-unchanged-files-writes-nothing, a-file-that-does-not-load-is-answered-and-nothing-is-written, an-edited-file-is-one-transaction-on-the-sessions-branch."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/an-import-of-unchanged-files-writes-nothing seon.system-test/a-file-that-does-not-load-is-answered-and-nothing-is-written seon.system-test/an-edited-file-is-one-transaction-on-the-sessions-branch}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-landing-restarts-root-only-for-a-binary-change"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner: Seon restarts only for a binary change; a schema change never needs a restart (Q2); \"I want the tree to be in the database and for us to build and run from the database\". What exists [M]: forms, config and schema entries load in place by the landing's own commit (restart-for and loads-in-place?, system.clj:1531-1560, 9cfddd6dc4); a landed schema file installs its new attributes in that write (changed-tx, system.clj:1292, d10bbb1b44); my.root/landed! (root.clj:435) answers {:kept} with :build when the binary is not current and bin/update builds with no restart; bin/land bridges by evaluating seon.system/landed-tx positionally as a code string in the running root (bin/land:38-62; landed-tx has two arities, system.clj:1359), so a new argument breaks an older root (deps' pin landing, 2026-11-14). Cause: four ways of computing what changed (load-changes!, load-diff!, changed-tx's class branches with fx/tree-diff, the bin/land bridge) and a path list (build-input?'s #{deps.edn bin/build .gitmodules}, system.clj:1570) deciding restarts. Design (one derivation, agreed 2026-11-14): the landing is one catch-up (seed, import and landing the same function), its pins stated as the whole set; the running root follows main by the program reaction (load-diff! from the program it holds), whose answer is :loaded or :restart, :restart only for a pin or binary change read from deps' seon.fx/runtime-for (a digest of build inputs vs target/seon's); a proc whose describe, params or state shape changed is swapped alone through Flow's update! (step fns are held by Var, graph.clj:46); bin/update then restarts on the new binary, later the online takeover (resume task). Deletes: the bin/land code-string bridge and landed-tx's positional call, restart-for's classes and loads-in-place?, build-input?'s path set, fx/tree-diff and fx/tree-file, judged-path's tree cache, runs/root-inputs.edn (bin/system:28-51). Never removes functionality: each caller moves to the catch-up. Estimate: a src-only landing costs load-diff! of its changed forms (~2.5M ticks a form) and no restart; a pin landing one restart (~0.2 s stop plus a boot). Proofs, red first: seon.system-test/a-pin-change-is-the-one-binary-restart; seon.system-test/a-proc-whose-ports-change-is-swapped; seon.system-test/an-older-root-takes-a-newer-landing (the handoff an old root reads); design guard seon.main-test/no-script-evaluates-code-in-root (no bin/ script sends a code string to root's eval)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-proc-whose-ports-change-is-swapped seon.system-test/a-pin-change-is-the-one-binary-restart seon.system-test/an-older-root-takes-a-newer-landing seon.main-test/no-script-evaluates-code-in-root}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-landing-that-adds-a-proc-restarts-before-its-code-loads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by web-2, 2026-11-14 22:52 [M]: the landing 1f796c16c8 added an fx kind (:server-op in resources/seon/system.edn) and code that uses it (seon.server/serve-connection! asks seon.system/effect! :server-op).\n- The bridge loaded the code into the running root first.\n- bin/update's (my.root/landed! ...) request then came in through the new serve-connection!, which asked for a kind the running graph didn't hold yet, and was answered :seon.fx/unknown-kind.\n- bin/update read that as a restart and restarted the standing root, its agents with it (pid 78915).\nThe class: a live load can load code that needs a change to the graph (a new proc or fx kind in system.edn) that only a restart brings. The restart decision should come before any code loads, from what the landing changed (system.edn's procs, fx kinds and conns, as restart-for already judges pins and binaries). Then the restart is planned and said (\"restart: the graph's procs changed\"), and no request runs half-new code.\nOwner: runtime-2 (the landing path: the bridge, my.root/landed!, seon.system/restart-for).\nProof: seon.system-test/a-landing-that-changes-the-graph-restarts-before-loading-its-code. A landing that adds an fx kind and a caller of it answers its update with a restart naming the graph, and no request answers :seon.fx/unknown-kind.\n\nOwner, root 22:54: runtime-2, as part of its one catch-up. The catch-up decides load-live-or-restart from what the landing changed (a new proc or fx kind in system.edn means restart first), before any code loads.\n\nAmended by runtime-2, 23:20 (taken into its one catch-up): a new proc or fx kind needs no restart, since Flow adds procs live (seon.system/apply! of the branch's system.edn, update!). The catch-up orders a landing's changes instead: first the declaration (apply!, live), then config and schemas, then the forms that call them. A restart stays only for a binary change (deps' digest). Proof, red first: a landing that adds an fx kind and its caller answers the caller's first request with the kind present.\n\nThe schema case, data-2, 23:58 [M] (one task with this one, root 23:54): the landing eb2dc776e0 added the malli host type :seon.contract/once (seon.contract/host-schemas, code) and a schema entry using it (:seon.db/entity-index, resources/seon/schemas/seon.core.edn). The bridge was refused at the standing root, \"malli has no schema :seon.contract/once\": changed-tx's decls step (src/seon/system.clj) compiles the landed schema files against the running registry, whose host types are its old code's, before any landed form loads; main moved in git, root was not updated. an-older-root-takes-a-newer-landing missed it because its test root runs the new code. The one catch-up's order covers it: the code a landing's schemas name loads before the schema step compiles them. Estimate: one set compare of the registry's host types against the landed entries' types, O(changed schema entries). Proof, red first: seon.system-test/a-landing-that-adds-a-host-type-enters-an-older-root, an older root (the host type absent from its registry) takes a landing that adds the host type and an entry using it, and serves the entry after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-landing-that-adds-a-host-type-enters-an-older-root seon.system-test/a-landing-that-changes-the-graph-restarts-before-loading-its-code}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-update-loses-nothing"
  :seon.task/plan "THE PLAN item 12. Owner's rulings (2026-11-15): an in-flight eval ends and resumes at its turn boundary from its branch (no root adopts another root's children); flights and requests are facts; nothing of ours is killed on wall time, only one spec'd stall dial for native code. So the design (audit target/root/audits-2026-11-15/processes-and-tests.md section 4.7): (a) each reconciler flight a fact {task step request} on main, re-issued idempotently by the next root (judges and verdicts reuse by digest); (b) a :running task of the old boot resumed, parked then woken (boot-wakes, load-diff!), never reaped :lost (reconcile.clj:870); (c) prove and :done requests facts keyed by run-key and task, so a client's re-ask after EOF reattaches; (d) the binary switch: build, boot the new binary on a clone, prove main through it, then stop the old root and start the new one; (e) one restart predicate (restart-for over baked-changes; loads-in-place? and build-input? collapse into it, with deps' runtime-for). Estimate to write per part before building; the proof below is the whole. THE PLAN M4, updates lose nothing (Stage 1's third safety item; root 2026-11-15). What a restart loses today [M, read in code, runtime-2 2026-11-15]: (1) every agent process: an agent's transport ends the process when root's socket end goes (seon.agent/await-reply: root gone ends the process); (2) the reconciler's in-memory :flights: a :running task comes back :lost, a model agent's work re-spawns on its branch, an outside agent's waits for a re-attach (seon.reconcile pass, the outside? clause); (3) every server op in flight: its connection drops, so a :done's answer is lost though its judge may still run. What a switch-over needs, none of which exists [M]: agents that outlive root's socket and reconnect to the next root (the socket is root's end of a socketpair the fork server passed); flights re-derived from the db at boot, not held only in memory (the judge's request, a spawn, a merge); clients that re-ask an op whose connection dropped. A pin then builds the next binary and switches to it with agents re-attaching, and the fork server loads only changed forms (seon.program/load-all!'s id-first path, root's half done in 7d4f5c9858). Deletes, when done: holds over restarts and the tree-compare stale-binary check (deps' runtime-for half). Done when: a pin lands during a watched seon_done and it ends green. Owner: runtime, deps for the binary's identity. Estimate and proofs to write before building."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-pin-during-a-watched-done-ends-green}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-import-never-records-a-file-it-did-not-write"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-15 by context's round 1 [M, root's REPL]: main held writer.clj's merge-main-into calling task/merged-cites-tx, but never task.clj's defn of it (data's ccdbab9b36; the var's history on main is empty until runtime-2's repair at t 536888864). One import took writer.clj from that commit and left task.clj out, yet main's :seon.system/source recorded the whole tree (a8cd7a03, digest 082fe4f4) as imported, and every agent merging main failed (No such var). Which step skipped task.clj is not established: no log line names it, and no merged task holds it unsynced now. Two defects [M]: (1) seon.system/import-of compares a read's digest, the whole tree's id (source-digest), with the held one, so my.root/import! with :paths for one file answers nothing to import whatever main lacks: a targeted re-import cannot repair (the repair went around it, through seon.source/tx-of and the writer); (2) an import or landing bridge that leaves a file out (catch-up's left-out kinds, a refused part) still records the tree, so the file is never read again. Design to write: a :paths import compares the files it read against main's forms, never the tree digest; and the source fact names what was left out of its tree, which the next import reads first. Proofs, red first: a :paths import of a file whose form main lacks writes that form; a left-out file is read again by the next import. Sibling sweep (root, 2026-11-15) [M, 199M ticks against 300M estimated]: every src file of the source tree compared form by form (text, so id) with main live forms: 69 files, 1 more mismatched, src/seon/contract.clj (checked-once, landed in git 865a75bc58 at 22:21, and the newer host-schemas: never on main). Speculated (+2 forms, -1 superseded, +1 var, none removed), then written through the writer at t 536888885. test/ and resources/ not swept. Owner: runtime (the catch-up and the bridge)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-paths-import-writes-what-main-lacks seon.system-test/a-left-out-file-is-read-again}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-run-stops-at-a-superseded-build"
  :seon.task/plan "Curated 2026-11-15 by context (root's ruling: a task's work is never a bin/ shell script; bin/ is a thin wrapper over an op, AGENTS §0): the work is the :build effect (seon.fx/build) answering a superseded build as a typed error its caller stops on, not bin/build's exit status. Measured by deps (2026-11-15 09:22): a bin/test run whose build was superseded (the worktree edited mid-build) ran 1,468 tests on the stale binary into env-mismatch, where it should have stopped. Cause [read]: bin/build prints SUPERSEDED and returns success (bin/build:350), and bin/test goes on to its shards; tests-4 met the same class as a red (seon.check-test/a-failed-seed-says-failed answered stale binary after an edit mid-run). The existing mechanism: seon.system/binary-current, the one owner of whether the binary is this tree's. Fix inside it, never another shell check: every runner (bin/test's plan, a test child, the test service) asks binary-current before a test that reaches the binary runs, and a superseded or stale binary is a typed error (:seon.system/stale-binary) that ends the run before any test, naming the tree it holds and the tree asked. Cost: one tree id compare a run, O(1). Proof, red first: a run whose binary is not its tree's runs no test and answers the typed error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.system-test/a-run-whose-binary-is-not-its-trees-runs-no-test}
  :seon.task/estimate #:seon.system{binary-current {:n 1, :order :constant, :ticks 100000, :bytes 100000}}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
